Then, for each possible value of $$m \in [1, k]$$ and $$n \in [m, k]$$, we can compute the value of $$f_{m, n}$$ (the number of ways to obtain $$$n$$$ using only integers from $$$1$$$ to $$$k$$$, except integer $$$x$$$) as follows:

$$
f_{m, n} = \begin{cases}
0 & (n = x) \\
1 & (n = m) \\
f_{m-1, n-m} & (n > m)
\end{cases}
$$

This formula is based on the following observations:

* If $$n = x$$, then there is no way to obtain it using only integers from 1 to k, except integer x.
* If $$n = m$$, then there is one way to obtain it using only integer m.
* If $$n > m$$, then there are $$f_{m-1, n-m}$$ ways to obtain it using only integers from 1 to k, except integer x.

We can then use the following recurrence relation:

$$
f_{m, n} = \begin{cases}
0 & (n = x) \\
f_{m, n-1} + f_{m-1, n-m} & (n > m)
\end{cases}
$$

This recurrence relation is based on the fact that we can either add integer m to the current sum, or we can add integer m-1 to the current sum and use integer m.

Finally, to determine whether it is possible to obtain $$$n$$$ using only integers from 1 to $$$k$$$, we can simply check whether $$f_{k, n}$$ is non-zero.

