This solution first counts the number of lamps that are pointing to the left and the number of lamps that are pointing to the right. If there are more lamps pointing to the left than to the right, it means that some trophies are not illuminated, and there is no way to illuminate them all. In that case, we return -1.

If there are more lamps pointing to the right than to the left, we can swap the direction of the two lamps that are pointing to each other, and this will illuminate all the trophies. In this case, we return the index of the lamp that is swapped.

If the number of lamps pointing to the left is equal to the number of lamps pointing to the right, we can illuminate all the trophies, and we return 0.

Note that this solution can be optimized further by considering the case where n is even and there is a lamp in the middle that is not pointing to any trophy. In this case, we can swap the direction of the lamp in the middle, and this will illuminate all the trophies.

